    "result_text": "The change is complete and the full test suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec required rejecting a supplied `registrationDate` later than the server date with a **400**. The `spring-petclinic-rest` app previously accepted future dates and silently persisted them.\n\n**Change** \u2014 `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`:\n\n1. Added a `rejectFutureRegistrationDate(...)` guard in the `addOwner(...)` flow (right after the existing capacity/identity checks, before the date is business-day-adjusted). It compares the *raw* supplied date against `LocalDate.now()`:\n\n```java\nprivate void rejectFutureRegistrationDate(LocalDate suppliedRegistrationDate) {\n    if (suppliedRegistrationDate != null && suppliedRegistrationDate.isAfter(LocalDate.now())) {\n        throw new InvalidOwnerFieldsException(List.of(\"registrationDate\"));\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThis reuses the existing `InvalidOwnerFieldsException`, which the `@ControllerAdvice` (`ExceptionControllerAdvice`) already maps to a `400 Bad Request` with the offending field name in the `errors` array \u2014 matching the codebase's established pattern for the other manual field guards. An omitted (`null`) date still defaults to the server date, and any date on or before today is accepted, so existing behavior is preserved.\n\n**Tests**: `mvn test` \u2192 `BUILD SUCCESS`, all tests pass (including the acceptance test `errorRejectsFutureDate`, which posts `registrationDate: \"2999-01-01\"` and expects 400).",
